{"597061": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Acetaminophen (Tylenol)</p>\n<p>Acetaminophen is the most widely used medicine for pain and fever. Its structure explains how it behaves in water: a benzene ring that is nonpolar, plus a phenol O&ndash;H and an amide group (N&ndash;H and C=O) that are very polar.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of acetaminophen: a benzene ring with an OH group on one end and an NH&amp;ndash;C(=O)&amp;ndash;CH3 amide group on the opposite end.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -118 170 235\" width=\"145\" height=\"200\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"71.7\" y1=\"-95.7\" x2=\"65.1\" y2=\"-60.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"67.1\" y1=\"-62.6\" x2=\"86.1\" y2=\"-46.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"63.2\" y1=\"-58.0\" x2=\"82.2\" y2=\"-41.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"65.1\" y1=\"-60.3\" x2=\"41.5\" y2=\"-52.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"-37.3\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"-12.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"32.7\" y1=\"-14.9\" x2=\"63.9\" y2=\"3.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.7\" y1=\"-9.7\" x2=\"60.9\" y2=\"8.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"5.7\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"41.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"63.9\" y1=\"44.3\" x2=\"32.7\" y2=\"62.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"60.9\" y1=\"39.1\" x2=\"29.7\" y2=\"57.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"59.7\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"84.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"59.7\" x2=\"0.0\" y2=\"41.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"-3.0\" y1=\"41.7\" x2=\"-3.0\" y2=\"5.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"3.0\" y1=\"41.7\" x2=\"3.0\" y2=\"5.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"-12.3\" x2=\"0.0\" y2=\"5.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"92.5\" y=\"-30.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"31.2\" y=\"-41.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"39.2\" y=\"-41.8\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"31.2\" y=\"102.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"39.2\" y=\"102.2\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of acetaminophen: a benzene ring with an OH group on one end and an NH&ndash;C(=O)&ndash;CH3 amide group on the opposite end. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Which intermolecular forces can acetaminophen form with water? Name the hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>All three: dispersion forces, dipole&ndash;dipole forces and <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong>. The H on the O&ndash;H and the H on the N&ndash;H are donors; the O of the OH and the carbonyl O are acceptors. The nonpolar ring is why acetaminophen is only moderately soluble in water rather than very soluble.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597072": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Halothane</p>\n<p>Halothane, CF<sub>3</sub>CHBrCl, was a standard inhaled general anesthetic for decades. In the United States it has been replaced by newer halogenated anesthetics such as sevoflurane, but it is still used in some parts of the world.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of halothane: a two-carbon chain; one carbon carries three F atoms, the other carries a Br and a Cl.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -64 166 128\" width=\"141\" height=\"109\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"9.0\" y1=\"-0.2\" x2=\"29.4\" y2=\"-14.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.4\" y1=\"-14.6\" x2=\"13.3\" y2=\"-33.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.4\" y1=\"-14.6\" x2=\"45.5\" y2=\"-33.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.4\" y1=\"-14.6\" x2=\"58.8\" y2=\"6.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"58.8\" y1=\"6.2\" x2=\"79.2\" y2=\"-8.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"58.8\" y1=\"6.2\" x2=\"58.8\" y2=\"31.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"0.0\" y=\"12.7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2e8b57\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >F</text><text x=\"6.3\" y=\"-35.7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2e8b57\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >F</text><text x=\"52.5\" y=\"-35.7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2e8b57\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >F</text><text x=\"88.2\" y=\"-8.1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2e8b57\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >Cl</text><text x=\"58.8\" y=\"48.7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8a5a1c\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >Br</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of halothane: a two-carbon chain; one carbon carries three F atoms, the other carries a Br and a Cl. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Give the IUPAC name of halothane.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div><strong>2-bromo-2-chloro-1,1,1-trifluoroethane.</strong> Two carbons make it an ethane; the halogens are named as substituents in alphabetical order (bromo, chloro, fluoro), numbered so the set of locants is lowest.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Why does a small halogenated hydrocarbon reach the brain so quickly?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>It is small and has low polarity, so it dissolves easily in the nonpolar lipid interior of cell membranes, including the membranes of nerve cells.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597082": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Methyl methacrylate (acrylic resin)</p>\n<p>Methyl methacrylate is the monomer of poly(methyl methacrylate), PMMA, the acrylic used for denture bases, custom impression trays and orthopedic bone cement. In the dental lab a liquid monomer is mixed with polymer powder and the C=C double bonds link up by addition polymerization.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of methyl methacrylate: CH2=C(CH3)&amp;ndash;C(=O)&amp;ndash;O&amp;ndash;CH3, a C=C double bond next to an ester group.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -67 199 134\" width=\"169\" height=\"114\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"-1.5\" y1=\"6.5\" x2=\"29.7\" y2=\"-11.5\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"1.5\" y1=\"11.7\" x2=\"32.7\" y2=\"-6.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"-8.9\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"-44.9\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"-8.9\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"9.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"65.3\" y1=\"8.8\" x2=\"67.6\" y2=\"33.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"59.4\" y1=\"9.4\" x2=\"61.7\" y2=\"34.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"9.1\" x2=\"81.4\" y2=\"-7.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"99.3\" y1=\"-8.8\" x2=\"120.9\" y2=\"3.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"65.6\" y=\"51.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"89.7\" y=\"-7.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of methyl methacrylate: CH2=C(CH3)&ndash;C(=O)&ndash;O&ndash;CH3, a C=C double bond next to an ester group. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Draw the repeat unit of PMMA. Which bond in the monomer is used up?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>The C=C double bond opens up: the repeat unit is <strong>&ndash;CH<sub>2</sub>&ndash;C(CH<sub>3</sub>)(COOCH<sub>3</sub>)&ndash;</strong>. The ester group is not part of the chain; it hangs off every other carbon as a side group.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597096": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Eugenol (oil of cloves)</p>\n<p>Eugenol is the main compound in clove oil. Mixed with zinc oxide it makes zinc oxide&ndash;eugenol (ZOE), a temporary filling and sedative dressing that soothes an irritated tooth pulp. Eugenol is also why a clove can numb a toothache.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of eugenol: a benzene ring with an OH group, an OCH3 group on the neighboring carbon, and a CH2&amp;ndash;CH=CH2 side chain.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -82 264 164\" width=\"224\" height=\"139\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"157.6\" y1=\"-60.0\" x2=\"134.0\" y2=\"-51.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"123.7\" y1=\"-37.0\" x2=\"123.7\" y2=\"-12.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"125.2\" y1=\"-14.6\" x2=\"156.3\" y2=\"3.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"122.2\" y1=\"-9.4\" x2=\"153.3\" y2=\"8.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"154.8\" y1=\"6.0\" x2=\"176.5\" y2=\"-6.5\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"154.8\" y1=\"6.0\" x2=\"154.8\" y2=\"42.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"156.3\" y1=\"44.6\" x2=\"125.2\" y2=\"62.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"153.3\" y1=\"39.4\" x2=\"122.2\" y2=\"57.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"123.7\" y1=\"60.0\" x2=\"92.5\" y2=\"42.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"92.5\" y1=\"42.0\" x2=\"61.3\" y2=\"60.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"61.3\" y1=\"60.0\" x2=\"33.9\" y2=\"36.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"34.9\" y1=\"39.5\" x2=\"1.0\" y2=\"51.5\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"32.9\" y1=\"33.8\" x2=\"-1.0\" y2=\"45.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"89.5\" y1=\"42.0\" x2=\"89.5\" y2=\"6.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"95.5\" y1=\"42.0\" x2=\"95.5\" y2=\"6.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"123.7\" y1=\"-12.0\" x2=\"92.5\" y2=\"6.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"123.7\" y=\"-41.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"186.0\" y=\"-5.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"194.0\" y=\"-5.5\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of eugenol: a benzene ring with an OH group, an OCH3 group on the neighboring carbon, and a CH2&ndash;CH=CH2 side chain. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Name every functional group in eugenol.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>A <strong>phenol</strong> (OH directly on the benzene ring), an <strong>ether</strong> (the methoxy group, OCH<sub>3</sub>), an <strong>alkene</strong> (the C=C in the side chain) and the <strong>aromatic ring</strong> itself.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597103": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Glutaraldehyde (a cold sterilant)</p>\n<p>Glutaraldehyde is a high-level disinfectant used for instruments that cannot be heated in an autoclave, such as endoscopes. Its two aldehyde groups react with the amino groups of microbial proteins and cross-link them, which kills bacteria, viruses and spores.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of glutaraldehyde: a five-carbon chain with an aldehyde group (CHO) at each end.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -36 260 73\" width=\"221\" height=\"62\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"8.0\" y1=\"6.3\" x2=\"29.7\" y2=\"-6.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"11.0\" y1=\"11.5\" x2=\"32.7\" y2=\"-1.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"-3.6\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"14.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"14.4\" x2=\"89.7\" y2=\"-9.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"89.7\" y1=\"-9.0\" x2=\"120.9\" y2=\"9.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"120.9\" y1=\"9.0\" x2=\"148.3\" y2=\"-14.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"149.3\" y1=\"-17.2\" x2=\"172.8\" y2=\"-8.9\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"147.3\" y1=\"-11.6\" x2=\"170.8\" y2=\"-3.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"0.0\" y=\"20.9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"182.2\" y=\"4.1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of glutaraldehyde: a five-carbon chain with an aldehyde group (CHO) at each end. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Give the IUPAC name of glutaraldehyde.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div><strong>Pentanedial</strong>: five carbons, two aldehyde groups (the ending &ldquo;-dial&rdquo;), both on chain ends so no numbers are needed.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Why must staff wear gloves and work with good ventilation when they use it?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>The same reaction that cross-links microbial proteins also attacks the proteins of skin and airways, so glutaraldehyde irritates them and can cause allergic sensitization.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597116": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Aspirin</p>\n<p>An old bottle of aspirin often smells like vinegar. Moisture slowly hydrolyzes the ester in aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), and one of the products is acetic acid, the acid in vinegar.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of aspirin: a benzene ring carrying a carboxylic acid group (COOH) and, on the neighboring carbon, an ester group O&amp;ndash;C(=O)&amp;ndash;CH3.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -100 233 199\" width=\"198\" height=\"170\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"134.0\" y1=\"-77.7\" x2=\"127.5\" y2=\"-42.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"129.4\" y1=\"-44.6\" x2=\"148.4\" y2=\"-28.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"125.5\" y1=\"-40.0\" x2=\"144.5\" y2=\"-23.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"127.5\" y1=\"-42.3\" x2=\"103.9\" y2=\"-34.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"-19.3\" x2=\"93.5\" y2=\"5.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"95.0\" y1=\"3.1\" x2=\"126.2\" y2=\"21.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"92.0\" y1=\"8.3\" x2=\"123.2\" y2=\"26.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"124.7\" y1=\"23.7\" x2=\"124.7\" y2=\"59.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"126.2\" y1=\"62.3\" x2=\"95.0\" y2=\"80.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"123.2\" y1=\"57.1\" x2=\"92.0\" y2=\"75.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"77.7\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"59.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"59.4\" y1=\"59.7\" x2=\"59.4\" y2=\"23.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"65.4\" y1=\"59.7\" x2=\"65.4\" y2=\"23.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"5.7\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"23.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"23.7\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"5.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"32.7\" y1=\"8.3\" x2=\"11.0\" y2=\"20.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.7\" y1=\"3.1\" x2=\"8.0\" y2=\"15.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"5.7\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"-19.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"154.8\" y=\"-12.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"93.5\" y=\"-23.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"0.0\" y=\"30.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"31.2\" y=\"-23.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"39.2\" y=\"-23.8\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of aspirin: a benzene ring carrying a carboxylic acid group (COOH) and, on the neighboring carbon, an ester group O&ndash;C(=O)&ndash;CH3. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Identify the functional groups in aspirin and write the products of its hydrolysis.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Aspirin has a <strong>carboxylic acid</strong> and an <strong>ester</strong> on a benzene ring. Hydrolysis of the ester gives <strong>salicylic acid</strong> (2-hydroxybenzoic acid) and <strong>acetic acid</strong>.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597131": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Epinephrine in a dental cartridge</p>\n<p>Dental anesthetic cartridges such as &ldquo;lidocaine with epinephrine 1:100,000&rdquo; contain epinephrine (adrenaline). It constricts nearby blood vessels, so the anesthetic stays at the site longer and there is less bleeding. Epinephrine is also the hormone and neurotransmitter of the fight-or-flight response.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of epinephrine: a benzene ring with two neighboring OH groups, attached to a CH(OH)&amp;ndash;CH2&amp;ndash;NH&amp;ndash;CH3 side chain.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -130 196 259\" width=\"167\" height=\"220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"0.0\" y1=\"-107.7\" x2=\"21.7\" y2=\"-95.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.2\" y1=\"-78.9\" x2=\"24.6\" y2=\"-54.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"24.6\" y1=\"-54.3\" x2=\"55.8\" y2=\"-36.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"55.8\" y1=\"-36.3\" x2=\"77.4\" y2=\"-48.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"55.8\" y1=\"-36.3\" x2=\"55.8\" y2=\"-0.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"57.3\" y1=\"-2.9\" x2=\"88.5\" y2=\"15.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"54.3\" y1=\"2.3\" x2=\"85.5\" y2=\"20.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"87.0\" y1=\"17.7\" x2=\"87.0\" y2=\"53.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"87.0\" y1=\"53.7\" x2=\"108.6\" y2=\"66.2\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"88.5\" y1=\"56.3\" x2=\"57.3\" y2=\"74.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"85.5\" y1=\"51.1\" x2=\"54.3\" y2=\"69.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"55.8\" y1=\"71.7\" x2=\"55.8\" y2=\"96.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"55.8\" y1=\"71.7\" x2=\"24.6\" y2=\"53.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"21.6\" y1=\"53.7\" x2=\"21.6\" y2=\"17.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"27.6\" y1=\"53.7\" x2=\"27.6\" y2=\"17.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"55.8\" y1=\"-0.3\" x2=\"24.6\" y2=\"17.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"31.2\" y=\"-83.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"39.2\" y=\"-83.2\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"87.0\" y=\"-47.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"95.0\" y=\"-47.8\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"118.1\" y=\"78.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"126.1\" y=\"78.2\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"55.8\" y=\"114.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"63.8\" y=\"114.2\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of epinephrine: a benzene ring with two neighboring OH groups, attached to a CH(OH)&ndash;CH2&ndash;NH&ndash;CH3 side chain. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Classify the amine in epinephrine, and name its other functional groups.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>The nitrogen carries two carbons, so it is a <strong>secondary amine</strong>. The two OH groups on the ring are <strong>phenols</strong>, and the OH on the side chain is a <strong>secondary alcohol</strong>.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Does epinephrine have a chiral carbon?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Yes: the side-chain carbon bonded to the OH has four different groups (H, OH, the ring and CH<sub>2</sub>NHCH<sub>3</sub>). The body makes and responds to one specific enantiomer.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597139": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Lactulose</p>\n<p>Lactulose is a synthetic disaccharide: galactose joined by a &beta;(1&rarr;4) glycosidic bond to fructose. Humans have no enzyme that hydrolyzes it, so it reaches the colon intact. There, bacteria ferment it into acids that pull water into the colon (a laxative effect) and trap ammonia as ammonium ion. That second effect is why lactulose is given to patients with liver failure and high blood ammonia.</p>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Lactose is galactose &beta;(1&rarr;4) glucose. How does lactulose differ, and why does it not raise blood glucose?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Lactulose has fructose where lactose has glucose. Because no human enzyme can hydrolyze it, it is never broken into absorbable monosaccharides, so nothing enters the blood.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Is lactulose a reducing sugar?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Yes. The galactose anomeric carbon is tied up in the glycosidic bond, but the anomeric carbon of the fructose unit is free, so the ring can open.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597146": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Propofol, the &ldquo;milky&rdquo; anesthetic</p>\n<p>Propofol is the most common drug for starting general anesthesia and for procedural sedation. It is a phenol that barely dissolves in water, so it is given IV as a white oil-in-water emulsion of soybean oil, glycerol and egg lecithin. Because the emulsion is also a good food for bacteria, opened vials must be used promptly or discarded.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of propofol: a benzene ring with an OH group flanked on both neighboring carbons by isopropyl groups.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -103 203 206\" width=\"172\" height=\"175\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"-81.0\" x2=\"93.5\" y2=\"-63.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"-63.0\" x2=\"124.7\" y2=\"-81.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"-63.0\" x2=\"93.5\" y2=\"-27.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"95.0\" y1=\"-29.6\" x2=\"126.2\" y2=\"-11.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"92.0\" y1=\"-24.4\" x2=\"123.2\" y2=\"-6.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"124.7\" y1=\"-9.0\" x2=\"124.7\" y2=\"27.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"126.2\" y1=\"29.6\" x2=\"95.0\" y2=\"47.6\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"123.2\" y1=\"24.4\" x2=\"92.0\" y2=\"42.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"45.0\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"27.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"27.0\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"45.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"45.0\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"81.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"45.0\" x2=\"0.0\" y2=\"27.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"59.4\" y1=\"27.0\" x2=\"59.4\" y2=\"-9.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"65.4\" y1=\"27.0\" x2=\"65.4\" y2=\"-9.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"93.5\" y1=\"-27.0\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"-9.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"-9.0\" x2=\"40.7\" y2=\"-21.5\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"31.2\" y=\"-20.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"23.2\" y=\"-20.5\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of propofol: a benzene ring with an OH group flanked on both neighboring carbons by isopropyl groups. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Lecithin is a glycerophospholipid. How does it keep the oil droplets dispersed in water?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Its two fatty-acid tails dissolve in the oil droplet and its charged phosphate&ndash;choline head faces the water, coating each droplet with a polar surface so the droplets do not merge.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597159": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Insulin</p>\n<p>Insulin is a small protein of 51 amino acids in two chains: an A chain of 21 and a B chain of 30, linked by two disulfide bonds, with a third disulfide inside the A chain. Rapid-acting &ldquo;insulin lispro&rdquo; differs from human insulin only in that the amino acids at positions B28 and B29 (proline and lysine) are swapped, which makes it cluster less and absorb faster after injection.</p>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> What bonds hold the A and B chains together, and which amino acid forms them?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Covalent <strong>disulfide bonds</strong> (&ndash;S&ndash;S&ndash;), formed between the side chains of two <strong>cysteine</strong> residues.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Swapping two amino acids in lispro changes which level of protein structure directly?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>The <strong>primary structure</strong>, the amino acid sequence. The change in how the molecules associate follows from it.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597169": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Amoxicillin and the &beta;-lactam ring</p>\n<p>Amoxicillin, a penicillin, is the most common antibiotic for dental infections. Every penicillin contains a strained four-membered cyclic amide called a &beta;-lactam. The bacterial enzyme that builds the cell wall attacks this ring and becomes covalently bonded to the drug, so it is permanently shut down and the bacterium bursts. Many resistant bacteria make &beta;-lactamase, an enzyme that hydrolyzes the ring first; Augmentin adds clavulanate to block that enzyme.</p>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Is penicillin a reversible or an irreversible inhibitor? Explain.</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div><strong>Irreversible</strong>: it forms a covalent bond in the enzyme&rsquo;s active site, so the enzyme cannot be recovered by adding more substrate.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> &beta;-Lactamase breaks the &beta;-lactam ring by hydrolysis. Which functional group is being hydrolyzed?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>An <strong>amide</strong> (the ring is a cyclic amide), the same reaction as amide hydrolysis in Chapter 7.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597181": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Acyclovir for cold sores</p>\n<p>Cold sores (herpes labialis), which dental hygienists see often, are treated with acyclovir. Acyclovir looks like the nucleoside guanosine, but its &ldquo;sugar&rdquo; is an open chain with no 3&prime;-OH. The viral enzyme thymidine kinase activates it, so it acts mainly in infected cells; the viral DNA polymerase then adds it to the growing DNA strand.</p>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Why does the growing viral DNA strand stop once acyclovir is added?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>The next nucleotide must form a phosphodiester bond to a <strong>3&prime;-OH</strong>, and acyclovir has none. The chain cannot be extended (chain termination).</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597197": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Metformin</p>\n<p>Metformin is the first-line drug for type 2 diabetes, and Rosa takes it. It lowers blood glucose mainly by decreasing the liver&rsquo;s glucose production, which is largely gluconeogenesis. It is not broken down in the body; the kidneys excrete it unchanged, which is why kidney function is checked before and during treatment.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of metformin: a dimethylamino group, N(CH3)2, attached to two linked C(=NH)&amp;ndash;NH units ending in NH2; a molecule with five nitrogen atoms.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -68 225 137\" width=\"191\" height=\"116\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"0.0\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"20.4\" y2=\"-4.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.4\" y1=\"-21.4\" x2=\"29.4\" y2=\"-46.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"38.4\" y1=\"-4.0\" x2=\"58.8\" y2=\"10.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"61.8\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"61.8\" y2=\"35.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"55.8\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"55.8\" y2=\"35.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"58.8\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"79.2\" y2=\"-4.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"97.2\" y1=\"-4.0\" x2=\"117.6\" y2=\"10.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"120.6\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"120.6\" y2=\"35.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"114.6\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"114.6\" y2=\"35.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"117.6\" y1=\"10.4\" x2=\"138.0\" y2=\"-4.0\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"29.4\" y=\"-3.9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"58.8\" y=\"52.9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"66.8\" y=\"52.9\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"88.2\" y=\"-3.9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"96.2\" y=\"-3.9\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"117.6\" y=\"52.9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"125.6\" y=\"52.9\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"147.0\" y=\"-3.9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >N</text><text x=\"155.0\" y=\"-3.9\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#2f5fc4\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H<tspan dy=\"5\" font-size=\"13\">2</tspan></text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of metformin: a dimethylamino group, N(CH3)2, attached to two linked C(=NH)&ndash;NH units ending in NH2; a molecule with five nitrogen atoms. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Is metformin water-soluble or fat-soluble? Why?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div><strong>Water-soluble.</strong> It is small, full of N&ndash;H groups that hydrogen-bond with water, and its basic nitrogens are protonated at body pH, so it is a cation.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Why does slowing gluconeogenesis lower a patient&rsquo;s <em>fasting</em> blood glucose in particular?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>Overnight, with no food coming in, the liver supplies blood glucose by glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis. Less gluconeogenesis means less glucose released while fasting.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597203": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Cyanide and its antidote</p>\n<p>Cyanide (CN<sup>&minus;</sup>), released when plastics, wool or silk burn, binds the iron of cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV). Electron transport stops, O<sub>2</sub> can no longer be used, and ATP production collapses. The antidote hydroxocobalamin is a form of vitamin B<sub>12</sub>: its cobalt binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, which the kidneys excrete (turning the urine red).</p>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> Blood lactate rises quickly in cyanide poisoning. Why?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>With Complex IV blocked, NADH cannot pass its electrons to O<sub>2</sub>, so NAD<sup>+</sup> is not regenerated by the chain. Cells keep glycolysis running by reducing pyruvate to lactate, which regenerates NAD<sup>+</sup>.</div></details>\n</div>\n", "597216": "<div class=\"box-note clinic-molecule\" style=\"border-left:6px solid #b03a2e;\">\n<p class=\"boxtitle\">Molecule in the Clinic: Sodium phenylbutyrate</p>\n<p>Children born with a urea cycle disorder cannot turn ammonia into urea. One treatment, sodium phenylbutyrate, gives the body another way out for nitrogen: it is converted to phenylacetate, which joins with glutamine to form phenylacetylglutamine, and that is excreted in the urine.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:300px;margin:.6em 0\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure of phenylbutyric acid: a benzene ring attached to a three-carbon chain ending in a carboxylic acid group (COOH). The drug is its sodium salt.\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" class=\"mol\" viewBox=\"-36 -124 204 247\" width=\"174\" height=\"210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skeletal structure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"103.6\" y1=\"-90.9\" x2=\"99.0\" y2=\"-66.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"101.0\" y1=\"-68.6\" x2=\"120.0\" y2=\"-52.4\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"97.1\" y1=\"-64.0\" x2=\"116.1\" y2=\"-47.8\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"99.0\" y1=\"-66.3\" x2=\"65.1\" y2=\"-54.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"65.1\" y1=\"-54.3\" x2=\"65.1\" y2=\"-18.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"65.1\" y1=\"-18.3\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"-6.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"-6.3\" x2=\"31.2\" y2=\"29.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"32.7\" y1=\"27.1\" x2=\"63.9\" y2=\"45.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"29.7\" y1=\"32.3\" x2=\"60.9\" y2=\"50.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"62.4\" y1=\"47.7\" x2=\"62.4\" y2=\"83.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"63.9\" y1=\"86.3\" x2=\"32.7\" y2=\"104.3\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"60.9\" y1=\"81.1\" x2=\"29.7\" y2=\"99.1\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"101.7\" x2=\"0.0\" y2=\"83.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"-3.0\" y1=\"83.7\" x2=\"-3.0\" y2=\"47.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"3.0\" y1=\"83.7\" x2=\"3.0\" y2=\"47.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"31.2\" y1=\"29.7\" x2=\"0.0\" y2=\"47.7\" stroke=\"#2d3f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"105.6\" y=\"-95.2\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text><text x=\"113.6\" y=\"-95.2\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >H</text><text x=\"126.4\" y=\"-36.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"800\" font-family=\"Inter,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" paint-order=\"stroke\" >O</text></svg><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;color:#555\">Skeletal structure of phenylbutyric acid: a benzene ring attached to a three-carbon chain ending in a carboxylic acid group (COOH). The drug is its sodium salt. Drawn with the Bond Voyage! engine (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> How many nitrogen atoms leave the body in each phenylacetylglutamine, and how does that compare with urea?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div><strong>Two</strong>, because glutamine carries two nitrogens (the amino group and the side-chain amide). Urea also carries two.</div></details>\n<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> What type of bond joins phenylacetate to glutamine&rsquo;s amino group?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><div>An <strong>amide</strong> bond, like a peptide bond (Chapter 7).</div></details>\n</div>\n"}